# Request Interceptor Extension - Development & Review Checklists
## Quality gates and verification steps for development workflow

## Pre-Development Checklist

### Before Starting Any Feature
- [ ] Read and understand the requirements
- [ ] Review related components in the codebase
- [ ] Check existing patterns in `speckit.patterns`
- [ ] Identify reusable UI components in `/src/components/ui/`
- [ ] Plan component structure and data flow
- [ ] Identify any new types needed in `/src/types/`
- [ ] Consider Chrome extension API requirements
- [ ] Plan testing approach

### Environment Setup
- [ ] Latest version of Node.js installed
- [ ] All dependencies installed (`pnpm install`)
- [ ] Development server runs without errors (`pnpm run dev`)
- [ ] TypeScript compilation succeeds (`pnpm run type-check`)
- [ ] Linting passes (`pnpm run lint`)
- [ ] Tests run successfully (`pnpm run test`)

## Development Checklist

### Component Development
- [ ] Component follows naming conventions (PascalCase, descriptive)
- [ ] Props interface properly typed with extending HTMLAttributes where applicable
- [ ] Default props assigned using destructuring with defaults
- [ ] Uses `cn()` utility for className composition
- [ ] Implements proper variant system if applicable
- [ ] Includes hover, focus, and disabled states
- [ ] Supports responsive design patterns
- [ ] Implements proper accessibility (ARIA labels, keyboard navigation)
- [ ] Uses Framer Motion for animations following established patterns
- [ ] Error boundaries implemented for complex components

### TypeScript Standards
- [ ] All variables and functions properly typed
- [ ] No `any` types used (use proper generics or union types)
- [ ] Interface names follow PascalCase convention
- [ ] Props interfaces extend appropriate HTML element types
- [ ] Complex types extracted to `/src/types/index.ts`
- [ ] Generic types used where appropriate
- [ ] Enums used instead of string unions where applicable

### State Management
- [ ] Zustand store structure follows established patterns
- [ ] State updates use immutable patterns
- [ ] Actions are pure functions
- [ ] Error handling included in async actions
- [ ] Notifications added for user feedback
- [ ] Store selectors optimized to prevent unnecessary re-renders
- [ ] Local state used only when appropriate (not everything needs global state)

### Styling & UI
- [ ] Tailwind classes organized in logical order (layout, spacing, colors, effects)
- [ ] Responsive design implemented mobile-first
- [ ] Dark mode considerations (if applicable)
- [ ] Consistent spacing using design tokens
- [ ] Proper color contrast ratios maintained
- [ ] Loading states implemented
- [ ] Empty states designed and implemented
- [ ] Error states handled gracefully

### Chrome Extension Specific
- [ ] Manifest permissions minimal and justified
- [ ] Background script memory usage optimized
- [ ] Message passing implemented correctly with error handling
- [ ] Chrome storage API used efficiently
- [ ] Content Security Policy compliance
- [ ] Extension popup/sidepanel sizing appropriate
- [ ] Cross-context communication tested

## Code Quality Checklist

### Code Structure
- [ ] Single responsibility principle followed
- [ ] Functions are small and focused (< 20 lines when possible)
- [ ] No deeply nested code (max 3-4 levels)
- [ ] Consistent naming throughout
- [ ] Magic numbers replaced with named constants
- [ ] Complex logic extracted to utility functions
- [ ] Dead code removed

### Performance
- [ ] `React.memo` used for expensive components
- [ ] `useMemo` and `useCallback` used appropriately
- [ ] Unnecessary re-renders eliminated
- [ ] Large lists implement virtual scrolling
- [ ] Images optimized and lazy loaded
- [ ] Bundle size impact assessed
- [ ] Memory leaks prevented (cleanup in useEffect)

### Error Handling
- [ ] Try-catch blocks for async operations
- [ ] User-friendly error messages
- [ ] Error boundaries implemented
- [ ] Chrome API errors handled gracefully
- [ ] Network errors handled appropriately
- [ ] Validation errors displayed to users
- [ ] Error logging for debugging

### Security
- [ ] Input sanitization implemented
- [ ] XSS prevention measures in place
- [ ] Content Security Policy compliant
- [ ] No sensitive data logged
- [ ] Chrome storage API used securely
- [ ] User permissions respected

## Testing Checklist

### Unit Tests
- [ ] All utility functions tested
- [ ] Component props variations tested
- [ ] User interaction scenarios covered
- [ ] Error conditions tested
- [ ] Edge cases identified and tested
- [ ] Mock dependencies appropriately
- [ ] Test coverage >80% for new code

### Integration Tests
- [ ] Component integration with stores tested
- [ ] Chrome API integration tested
- [ ] User workflows tested end-to-end
- [ ] Route interception functionality verified
- [ ] Cross-context communication tested

### Test Quality
- [ ] Test names clearly describe what is being tested
- [ ] Tests are independent and can run in isolation
- [ ] Test data is realistic and representative
- [ ] Async operations properly awaited
- [ ] Cleanup performed in tests
- [ ] No test pollution between tests

## Pre-Commit Checklist

### Code Verification
- [ ] TypeScript compilation passes (`pnpm run type-check`)
- [ ] ESLint passes with zero warnings (`pnpm run lint`)
- [ ] All tests pass (`pnpm run test:run`)
- [ ] Build succeeds (`pnpm run build`)
- [ ] Manual testing performed
- [ ] Browser extension loads without errors

### Code Review Preparation
- [ ] Commit messages are clear and descriptive
- [ ] Changes are logically grouped in commits
- [ ] No debugging code or console.logs left in
- [ ] Documentation updated for new features
- [ ] Breaking changes documented
- [ ] Screenshots/videos added for UI changes

### Files and Documentation
- [ ] New files follow naming conventions
- [ ] Imports organized correctly
- [ ] Unused imports removed
- [ ] File exports consistent
- [ ] README updated if necessary
- [ ] API documentation updated

## Code Review Checklist

### Functionality Review
- [ ] Feature works as intended
- [ ] Edge cases handled appropriately
- [ ] Error scenarios tested
- [ ] Performance impact acceptable
- [ ] Browser extension functionality verified
- [ ] No regressions introduced

### Code Quality Review
- [ ] Code follows established patterns
- [ ] Naming conventions followed
- [ ] Code is readable and maintainable
- [ ] No code duplication
- [ ] Appropriate abstractions used
- [ ] Security considerations addressed

### Testing Review
- [ ] Tests cover new functionality
- [ ] Test quality is high
- [ ] Test coverage adequate
- [ ] Tests run reliably
- [ ] Edge cases tested

### Documentation Review
- [ ] Code is self-documenting or properly commented
- [ ] Public APIs documented
- [ ] Complex logic explained
- [ ] Breaking changes noted
- [ ] Examples provided where helpful

## Release Checklist

### Pre-Release Verification
- [ ] All tests pass on CI/CD
- [ ] Manual testing completed
- [ ] Browser extension tested in multiple browsers
- [ ] Performance metrics acceptable
- [ ] Bundle size impact acceptable
- [ ] No critical security vulnerabilities

### Extension Store Preparation
- [ ] Manifest version updated
- [ ] Extension description updated
- [ ] Screenshots updated
- [ ] Privacy policy reviewed
- [ ] Permissions justified and documented
- [ ] Release notes prepared

### Deployment
- [ ] Version tagged in git
- [ ] Release notes published
- [ ] Extension uploaded to store
- [ ] Distribution files backed up
- [ ] Monitoring set up for new version

